{
  "id": "k8s-vault-auth",
  "provider": "kubernetes",
  "title": "HashiCorp Vault Kubernetes auth method",
  "summary": "A pod logs in to Vault with its service account token. Vault checks the token with the cluster, matches the service account to a Vault role, and returns a short-lived Vault token limited to that role's policies.",
  "notes": [
    "The Vault role binds service account names and namespaces (bound_service_account_names / bound_service_account_namespaces) to policies. That binding is the whole authorization decision.",
    "Vault calls the TokenReview API to check the token, so Vault needs network access to the API server and a reviewer identity with the system:auth-delegator role.",
    "Alternative: Vault's JWT auth method verifies the token offline using the cluster's OIDC discovery keys. Vault then needs no API access, but can't notice a deleted pod before the token expires.",
    "Use a projected token with Vault as the audience, not the default token, and keep the Vault token TTL short."
  ],
  "docs": [
    {
      "title": "Vault: Kubernetes auth method",
      "url": "https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/auth/kubernetes"
    },
    {
      "title": "Vault: JWT/OIDC auth method",
      "url": "https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/auth/jwt"
    },
    {
      "title": "Vault Agent Injector for Kubernetes",
      "url": "https://developer.hashicorp.com/vault/docs/platform/k8s/injector"
    }
  ],
  "graph": {
